1.34.0: the tone equalizer

The scene controls bend the tone curve in fixed ways: --ev slides it,
--contrast turns it, --shadows lifts its left half. There was no way to
reach one part of it and leave the rest. Now there is, built like a
parametric equalizer in audio, on the scale of scene brightness instead
of pitch: four bands, each a bell with a lift, a center and a width.

  --tone1 .. --tone4            lift (+) or cut (-) in stops, -2..2
  --tone1at .. --tone4at        center, stops from middle gray, -8..4
                                (resting at -4, -2, 0, +2)
  --tone1width .. --tone4width  width in stops, 0.3..3 (resting at 1)

In stops: out = in + sum_i lift_i exp(-(in - center_i)^2 / 2 width_i^2).

Where and how. After --ev, before everything else, so the film answers
a differently lit scene and the centers follow the middle gray chosen
with the exposure. It is a gain found from luminance and applied to R,
G and B alike: the equalizer moves no hue (ratios unchanged to four
digits, tested), and it does not edit a film LUT, whose "tone curve" is
only its gray axis - it composes with it.

Never reversing. A bell's steepest slope is 0.607 lift/width; past -1
the curve folds. A band is held to 1.48 stops per stop of width, and if
bands of opposite sign still add up to a fold all four are scaled back
by one factor; the steepest fall keeps a tenth of the slope. Settings
are not refused, they are held, and the holding is reported.

The page. A "Tone curve" group: the look's tone curve as the engine
computes it (new pj_tone_curve: a gray ramp through the look's color
path), dashed with the tone controls at rest and solid as set, with the
four bands as numbered handles on it. Drag sideways for the center, up
and down for the lift (a stop per 28 graph units, the same everywhere,
and no further than the width allows), wheel for the width,
double-click to flatten; "flatten all"; the twelve values as sliders
under "by numbers". The handles are numbered and the two curves differ
by dash, not color. The curve comes from the engine in both modes: a
new endpoint /api/tonecurve in serve, a "tonecurve" command in the
wasm, toneCurve() in both JavaScript engines.

Everywhere else. The twelve values are scene keys: recipes, superjuice,
serve queries, super8ify variables (TONE1 ..), completions, and - being
tone - exported LUTs and darktable styles. 'polajuice curves --table'
now ends with the tone curve by numbers (output for a gray at each half
stop, at rest and as set), the bands, and whether one was held.

Flat bands are no change to the bit wherever they sit; with none set,
output is byte-identical to 1.33.0. A recipe from before 1.34 has no
tone keys and reads as four flat bands (the page now also resets them,
and the grain keys, when such a recipe is loaded).

Limits raised to make room for twelve more keys: recipe text buffers
1024 -> 2048, a serve request's parameters 48 -> 96, a recipe file's
scene keys 48 -> 64, the wasm scene string 512 -> 1536.

The guide: section 6.15 with a diagram drawn from the engine's curve
and two examples; the page, style, equivalences, limits and option
tables updated. README: a section, and the annex says what the
equalizer is linked to (--ev, --contrast, --shadows: by construction,
none exactly) and that it adds freedom in the shape of one curve, not a
dimension of color.

Tests: core (keys and resting places, one band's gain against the
formula, ratios kept, flat bands bit-identical, the curve at rest, a
lift where the band is and nowhere far, handles shifted by exposure and
by raw, held bands reported and still rising, the JSON); CLI (flat
default, replay, pre-1.34 recipe, ranges, LUT carries it, the table);
serve (render equals the CLI, the curve endpoint); wasm; page; and in a
real browser, dragging a handle changes the recipe, the CLI line and
the curve, and flatten all restores them.
